{"title":"Warsaw, Missouri","text":"Warsaw, Missouri
Warsaw was platted in 1837 . The city was incorporated in 1843 and designated as the county seat of Benton County . The city was named after Warsaw , the capital city of Poland , in honor of the Revolutionary War hero Tadeusz Kościuszko , who was Polish and had also fought for independence in his homeland . Warsaw was a steamboat port , the furthest point up the Osage River they could navigate . Permanent immigrants to the area were Germans , Scotch - Irish , and English , as well as transplant farmers from Kentucky , Tennessee , and the Carolinas , first arriving around 1820 . The Upper Bridge was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1999 . Geography ( edit ) Warsaw is located at 38 ° 14'43 '' North , 93 ° 22'38 '' West ( 38.245195 , - 93.377227 ) .","entities":["Warsaw","Osage River","Platting of Warsaw","Poland","Tadeusz Kościuszko","Revolutionary War","Incorporation of Warsaw","Designation of Warsaw as county seat","Upper Bridge","National Register of Historic Places","Listing of Upper Bridge on the National Register of Historic Places","Permanent immigration to the area","Germans","Scotch-Irish","English"],"facts":["Temperature at Warsaw fell to -40 °F (-40 °C) -LOCATED_IN-> Warsaw","Osage -RELATED_TO-> Osage River","French settlers' visit -LOCATED_IN-> Warsaw","Temperature in Warsaw rose to 118 °F (48 °C) -LOCATED_IN-> Warsaw","National Register Information System -RELATED_TO-> National Register of Historic Places"]}

