{"title":"Governor-General of India","text":"Governor-General of India
The power to elect the Governor - General was retained by the Court of Directors , but the choice became subject to the Sovereign 's approval . After the Indian Rebellion of 1857 , the East India Company 's territories in India were put under the direct control of the Sovereign . The Government of India Act 1858 vested the power to appoint the Governor - General in the Sovereign . The Governor - General , in turn , had the power to appoint all lieutenant governors in India , subject to the Sovereign 's approval . India and Pakistan acquired independence in 1947 , but Governors - General continued to be appointed over each nation until republican constitutions were written . Louis Mountbatten , 1st Earl Mountbatten of Burma remained Governor - General of India for some time after independence , but the two nations were otherwise headed by native Governors - General . India became a secular republic in 1950 ; Pakistan became an Islamic one in 1956 . Functions ( edit ) Lord Curzon in his robes as Viceroy of India , a post he held from 1899 to 1905 .","entities":["Pakistan","republican constitutions","sovereign","Louis Mountbatten","India","Indian Rebellion of 1857","Government of India Act 1858","Independence of India and Pakistan","Native Governors-General","India became a secular republic","Secular republic","Pakistan became an Islamic republic","Islamic republic","Lord Curzon","East India Company"],"facts":["Governor-General of India -RELATED_TO-> Pakistan","Former Governors general -RELATED_TO-> Pakistan","Split of British India -RELATED_TO-> Pakistan","Pakistan -RELATED_TO-> Adoption of republican constitution in Pakistan","Governor-General of India -LOCATED_IN-> Pakistan"]}
