In January 2014 , the United States Senate passed the Homeowner Flood Insurance Affordability Act of 2014 ( S. 1926 ; 113th Congress ) . This bill changed the process used to alter subsidized premiums and reinstated grandfathering of lower rates ; effectively delaying the increases in flood insurance premiums to obtain risk - based premiums under Biggert - Waters and spreading the cost of the lost premiums over all of the remaining policy holders . The National Flood Insurance Program was $24 billion in debt at the beginning of 2014 as a result of Hurricanes Katrina , Rita and Sandy . The passage of the HFIAA described above has concerned insurance and environmental observers that the delay in implementation of actuarial rates will leave taxpayers exposed to additional losses . Floodplain status determination appeals and changes ( edit ) Letter of Map Amendment ( edit ) Insufficient map topographic detail or accuracy can result in the unwarranted determination of Special Flood Hazard Area ( SFHA ) . An application for a Letter of Map Amendment ( LOMA ) uses an Elevation Certificate ( prepared by a Registered Land Surveyor or Registered Professional Engineer ) to ask FEMA to remove the flood insurance requirement on individual properties .","entities":["National Flood Insurance Program","Hurricane Katrina","Hurricane Sandy","United States Senate","Homeowner Flood Insurance Affordability Act of 2014","Passage of the Homeowner Flood Insurance Affordability Act of 2014","Biggert-Waters","Hurricane Rita","Letter of Map Amendment","Elevation Certificate","Special Flood Hazard Area","FEMA"],"facts":["National Flood Insurance Program -RELATED_TO-> National Flood Insurance Act of 1968","National Flood Insurance Program -RELATED_TO-> U.S. Treasury","National Flood Insurance Program -RELATED_TO-> local communities","National Flood Insurance Program -RELATED_TO-> debt","National Flood Insurance Program -RELATED_TO-> premiums"]}
