If the MAC address is found , it sends an Ethernet frame with destination address 00 : eb : 24 : b2 : 05 : ac , containing the IP packet onto the link . If the cache did not produce a result for 192.168. 0.55 , Computer 1 has to send a broadcast ARP message ( destination FF : FF : FF : FF : FF : FF MAC address ) , which is accepted by all computers , requesting an answer for 192.168. 0.55 . Computer 2 responds with its MAC and IP addresses . Computer 2 may insert an entry for Computer 1 into its ARP table for future use . Computer 1 caches the response information in its ARP table and can now send the packet . ARP probe ( edit ) An ARP probe is an ARP request constructed with an all - zero sender IP address ( SPA ) . The term is used in the IPv4 Address Conflict Detection specification ( RFC 5227 ) .","entities":["MAC address","Computer 1","Computer 2","192.168. 0.55","00 : eb : 24 : b2 : 05 : ac","Ethernet frame","IP packet","FF : FF : FF : FF : FF : FF","ARP probe","zero sender IP address","IPv4 Address Conflict Detection specification","link","ARP table","ARP request","Address Resolution Protocol"],"facts":["link layer address -RELATED_TO-> MAC address","arp command -RELATED_TO-> MAC address","ARP announcements -RELATED_TO-> MAC address","team of network cards -RELATED_TO-> MAC address","Computer 1 -RELATED_TO-> local area network"]}
