It was a representation , in effect , as to the future , namely , that payment of the rent would not be enforced at the full rate but only at the reduced rate . Such a representation would not give rise to an estoppel , because , as was said in Jorden v. Money ( 1854 ) 5 H.L.C. 185 , a representation as to the future must be embodied as a contract or be nothing . But what is the position in view of developments in the law in recent years ? The law has not been standing still since Jorden v. Money ( 1854 ) 5 H.L.C. 185 . There has been a series of decisions over the last fifty years which , although they are said to be cases of estoppel are not really such . They are cases in which a promise was made which was intended to create legal relations and which , to the knowledge of the person making the promise , was going to be acted on by the person to whom it was made and which was in fact so acted on . In such cases the courts have said that the promise must be honoured .","entities":["Central London Property Trust Ltd","High Trees House Ltd","Jorden v. Money (1854) 5 H.L.C. 185","Jorden","Money","estoppel","contract","promise","rent","Central London Property Trust Ltd v High Trees House Ltd"],"facts":["Central London Property Trust Ltd -PARTICIPATED_IN-> lease of a block of flats","High Trees House Ltd -PARTICIPATED_IN-> agreement to reduce rent by half","Central London Property Trust Ltd -PARTICIPATED_IN-> agreement to reduce rent by half","High Trees House Ltd -PARTICIPATED_IN-> lease of a block of flats","estoppel -RELATED_TO-> representation"]}
