It is traversed by the squamosal , sphenoparietal , sphenosquamosal , and sphenopetrosal sutures . It houses the temporal lobes of the brain and the pituitary gland . A middle fossa craniotomy is one means to surgically remove acoustic neuromas ( vestibular schwannoma ) growing within the internal auditory canal of the temporal bone . Middle cranial fossa surgical anatomy as demonstrated in a right cadaver temporal bone by Dr Jack M Kartush - view from above . Contents ( hide ) 1 Middle part 2 Lateral parts 3 See also 4 References 5 Additional images 6 External links Middle part ( edit ) The middle part of the fossa presents , in front , the chiasmatic groove and tuberculum sellae ; the chiasmatic groove ends on either side at the optic foramen , which transmits the optic nerve and ophthalmic artery to the orbital cavity . Behind the optic foramen the anterior clinoid process is directed backward and medialward and gives attachment to the tentorium cerebelli .","entities":["Middle cranial fossa","squamosal suture","sphenoparietal suture","sphenosquamosal suture","sphenopetrosal suture","temporal lobes","brain","pituitary gland","middle fossa craniotomy","acoustic neuromas","internal auditory canal","temporal bone","Dr Jack M Kartush","middle part","chiasmatic groove"],"facts":["Middle cranial fossa -PART_OF-> skull","Lateral parts of the middle fossa -PART_OF-> Middle cranial fossa","Orbital branch of the middle meningeal artery -PART_OF-> Middle cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> anterior cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> posterior fossa"]}
{"title":"Middle cranial fossa","text":"Middle cranial fossa
Lateral parts ( edit ) The lateral parts of the middle fossa are of considerable depth , and support the temporal lobes of the brain . They are marked by depressions for the brain convolutions and traversed by furrows for the anterior and posterior branches of the middle meningeal vessels . These furrows begin near the foramen spinosum , and the anterior runs forward and upward to the sphenoidal angle of the parietal , where it is sometimes converted into a bony canal ; the posterior runs lateralward and backward across the temporal squama and passes on to the parietal near the middle of its lower border . The following apertures are also to be seen . In front is the superior orbital fissure , bounded above by the small wing , below , by the great wing , and medially , by the body of the sphenoid ; it is usually completed laterally by the orbital plate of the frontal bone .","entities":["Middle cranial fossa","temporal lobes","brain","Lateral parts of the middle fossa","Depressions","Brain convolutions","Anterior furrow","Posterior furrow","Anterior branch of the middle meningeal vessels","Posterior branch of the middle meningeal vessels","Middle meningeal vessels","Sphenoidal angle of the parietal","Parietal","Bony canal","Temporal squama"],"facts":["middle part -PART_OF-> Middle cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa -PART_OF-> skull","Orbital branch of the middle meningeal artery -PART_OF-> Middle cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> anterior cranial fossa","Middle cranial fossa -RELATED_TO-> posterior fossa"]}
